{"study_accession":"PRJNA1041580","secondary_study_accession":"SRP472472","title":"Off-label use of Baricitinib improves moderate and severe atopic dermatitis in China through inhibiting MAPK and PI3K/Akt/mTOR pathway via targeting JAK-STAT signaling of CD4+ cells","description":"As an inflammatory disease with a disrupted immune system, cytokine disorders in atopic dermatitis are closely related to the abnormal activation of JAK-STAT signal pathway. The critical relevance of the JAK-STAT signaling pathway to the pathogenesis of AD provides a strong rationale for JAK inhibitor research. Baricitinib, a small-molecule oral JAK inhibitor, was approved in the European Union in 2017 and is currently available in China for off-label use. However, the off-label use of Baricitinib for AD in China and its mechanism are rarely reported. In our study, we found that the immune status of patients with moderate and severe AD was hyperactive. Among the 49 known immunotherapy targets, JAK1 and JAK2 target genes on lymphocytes of AD patients were significantly up-regulated, and high JAK1/2 expression was closely related to the symptom severity in moderate and severe AD patients. Off-label use of JAK1/2-targeting inhibitor (Baricitinib) can improve immune hyperresponsiveness and clinical symptoms in moderate and severe AD by inhibiting the activation of Th2 cell subsets and the secretion of Th2-type cytokines.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":30,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN38288277","study_accession":"PRJNA1041580","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","description":"Human sample from Homo sapiens","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-03-24","attributes":[["isolate","Shuang Chen"],["age","missing"],["biomaterial_provider","The First Affiliated Hospital of Chongqing Medical University"],["collection_date","2021-04-30"],["geo_loc_name","China"],["sex","male"],["tissue